## What to look for

### A. Agent execution quality

The agent's own choices, whatever the request said. Cover only what the trace shows, and carry each
finding through to the change it implies.

- **Wrong instrument** — a call another available tool does directly and better. The change is a
  tool swap; an observation above already names which one, if the observations list one.
- **Redundant work** — the same ground covered twice. The `× N`, `repeat of call` and `same file,
  different input` markers are placed for exactly this, so quote the line carrying one. The change
  is a rule about how to read or search — never "be more careful".
- **Recovery after a failure** — for each FAILED or REJECTED call, and for any failed model calls
  the observations list, do the calls after it address that specific cause, or work around it
  blindly? The change is what should have been checked first. Only failures the timeline, the
  observations or the Errors section actually shows.
- **Disproportionate model work** — the outlier model call, if the observations list one and do not
  already excuse it. Whether that call's length was earned has been decided for you from its output
  rate: an outlier whose duration is explained by what it wrote carries a note above saying so, and
  is not yours to fault — however long it took, and however large its token count looks. One that
  reaches you without that note spent time it did not write anything for: it was deciding, and the
  change is to leave it less to decide — a narrower request, one job per turn instead of several at
  once. The observation names the model and effort level that call ran at: if the reasoning looks
  disproportionate for the model/effort shown (a cheap model or low effort spending this long
  deciding), naming a stronger model or higher effort is also a legitimate fix, alongside or instead
  of narrowing the request.
