### B. Request quality

The text under "User prompt" was written by a person; judge how well it set the agent up.
{{^wordingAlreadySettled}}

**First decide what kind of request this is; the two are judged differently and confusing them
produces the worst advice this review can give.** A request that does both is judged half by each.

- An **instruction** names work to do ("fix the cache-read total", "apply the handoff in `<path>`").
  Both faults below apply to it — but **a short instruction is not automatically an underspecified
  one.** "Commit and push", "fix the failing test", "run the linter and fix what it finds" name
  their target completely, because the target is the state the agent can already see; working out
  which files, which test, or what commit message from the repository in front of it is the agent's
  job, not a detail the reader left out. Judge by whether the agent had to guess at something it
  could not derive, never by how few words were used.
- A **question** asks for gaps, options, an opinion, or whether something is worth doing ("is there
  anything missing in `<file>`", "what are some other approaches"). **It is not underspecified for
  being open**: not knowing the answer is why it was asked, and producing that answer is what this
  trace was run to do. It has no target to name, so neither fault below applies to it. Whether the
  agent actually answered it is section A's business.

**A cost or duration figure is never evidence about how the request was worded.** A long model call,
a big output, an expensive subagent, a loaded context — those are facts about what the work took, and
they are section C's to report. Reaching from one of them to "the request was too open-ended" or "the
request asked for too much" is a guess wearing a number as evidence: the same figures appear on a
perfectly worded request for a genuinely large job. Judge the wording from the wording.

- **Unnamed target** — a call that only happened because the request did not say where to look.
  Quote the wording and the call it caused. This is about an instruction whose target went unsaid,
  never a question whose answer was unknown.
  **A request that named a user-visible surface named its target** — a page, a section, a control, an
  error message. That is the vocabulary a reader has, and the calls that found which files implement
  it are the agent doing its job, not a cost the wording imposed. **Never ask the reader to name a
  file, class or module unless the request shows they already knew it**; they could not have followed
  that. Raise this only for something the reader plainly had in mind and could have written — which
  of several things they meant, or a scope already decided.
