### C. Cost, time and context
{{#judgesCost}}

What this trace spent, and whether the shape of the request is why. Report only what the figures
above actually show; if none of these stands out, say nothing here.

- **What drove the cost** — read the spend split. If one sub-agent or one call carried most of it,
  name it and say whether the work it did was worth that share. A sub-agent that kept a long search
  out of the main context is money well spent; one dispatched to do something the agent could have
  done in two tool calls is not.
- **Where the time went** — compare LLM generation, tool execution and time blocked on the user. A
  trace dominated by tool execution points at slow or over-broad commands; one dominated by blocked
  time points at calls that needed approval and could have been batched or pre-authorised.
- **Cache reuse** — only if the observations flagged it as poor. Low reuse across many calls means
  the conversation kept invalidating its own cache; the change is usually to stop interleaving edits
  with re-reads of the same large files.
{{^isContinuation}}
- **Was this the right session?** — decide from the context-size line. A request that started on a
  large inherited context paid for that history on every call it made, and if the work it did was
  unrelated to what came before, the change is to start it in a fresh session. A context that grew
  steeply within one request usually means several jobs were asked for at once; the change is to
  split them across turns.
{{/isContinuation}}
